"Direct Determination of a Non-Cooperative LEO Satellite’s Orbital State from Ground-Station Signals: A Cramér–Rao Analysis - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13667748/)\nciteturn222search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: yesterday; Because the trajectory is a deterministic function of this state, every candidate is a valid orbit. ... The present work differs in three respects, which are stated here so that the novelty is not misread: (i) the estimand is the six-dimensional orbital state of the emitter with the receivers at known positions—the inverse of the receiver-localization problem—so that the satellite is treated exactly as a moving emitter in direct geolocation; (ii) the estimation is single-step, from the received samples, with the orbital dynamics built into the parameterization rather than imposed on extracted observables; and (iii) the Cramér–Rao bound is derived in a form that separates signal, geometry and dynamics, with the dynamics entering through a closed-form Keplerian state-transition block. ... The approach is distinct from angle-only non-cooperative orbit determination (which uses optical bearings and two-step filtering) and POD (cooperative two-step); it is the direct RF single-step counterpart aimed at the weak and uncooperative regime. ... The unknown-waveform direct estimator is therefore viable for strong narrowband or short captures (high ${\\rho }_{s}$), whereas, at low-pass SNRs where the single-step advantage of Section 7 is predicted, the waveform must be known or at least structured (a known modulation format, a pilot, or a cyclostationary signature), which is the usual situation for a non-cooperative but standard-compliant downlink.\n\n. 2026 Oct 6;26(19):6306. doi: 10.3390/s26196306\n\n# Direct Determination of a Non-Cooperative LEO Satellite’s Orbital State from Ground-Station Signals: A Cramér–Rao Analysis\n\nReceived 2026 Sep 4; Revised 2026 Sep 24; Accepted 2026 Oct 2; Collection date 2026 Oct.\n\n© 2026 by the author.\n\nLicensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license.\n\nPMC Copyright notice\n\nPMCID: PMC13667748  PMID: 42884506\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis paper considers passive determination of the orbital state of a non-cooperative low-Earth-orbit satellite from radio signals received at several ground stations. Reversing the roles of direct geolocation in which a satellite with known ephemeris locates a ground emitter, the satellite’s six-dimensional epoch state is estimated in a single step from the received data. Because the trajectory is a deterministic function of this state, every candidate is a valid orbit. The Cramér–Rao bound is derived; it factors into signal, geometry and orbital-dynamics terms, and a closed-form Keplerian state-transition block is obtained by implicit differentiation of the universal Kepler equation. The delay model includes the light-time correction, which leaves the bound essentially unchanged but removes a bias of more than a hundred meters. The unknown emission epoch is treated as a nuisance; it roughly doubles the three-station bound, whereas four stations recover the known-epoch accuracy. Simulations validate the dynamics implementation and demonstrate post-acquisition efficiency under a Gaussian delay/Doppler surrogate of the known-waveform model. Under an independent-cell model of acquisition failures, an ideal exhaustive search predicts a threshold about 6 dB below that of a two-step baseline with hard per-window peak decisions. The paper also quantifies how accuracy scales with arc length, station count and station layout.\n\nKeywords: direct position determination, orbital-state estimation, Cramér–Rao bound, LEO satellites, passive geolocation, non-cooperative emitters, two-body dynamics, state-transition matrix, space situational awareness\nDoppler-based opportunistic positioning from LEO downlinks, including differential schemes and the analysis of their baseline dependence, is developed in [20,21,22,23,24,25]; orbit determination and ephemeris tracking of non-cooperative satellites from Doppler observables extracted by a receiver at a known position, including the simultaneous tracking-and-navigation framework and its machine learning variants, in [26,27,28,29,30,31]; and the modeling and compensation of the timing and spatial ephemeris errors of such satellites in [32,33]. All of these are two-step: per-station delay and/or Doppler observables are extracted from the signal and an orbit or a receiver state is then fitted to them, with the extraction usually relying on knowledge of the signal structure (a beacon, a pilot, or a modulation format). The present work differs in three respects, which are stated here so that the novelty is not misread: (i) the estimand is the six-dimensional orbital state of the emitter with the receivers at known positions—the inverse of the receiver-localization problem—so that the satellite is treated exactly as a moving emitter in direct geolocation; (ii) the estimation is single-step, from the received samples, with the orbital dynamics built into the parameterization rather than imposed on extracted observables; and (iii) the Cramér–Rao bound is derived in a form that separates signal, geometry and dynamics, with the dynamics entering through a closed-form Keplerian state-transition block. The first two are what make the low-SNR behavior different from that of the two-step chain; the third is what makes the accuracy predictable. Direct processing of raw data (the DPD principle) and the inversion of roles are the setting rather than the novelty in themselves; the principal contributions are items (ii) and (iii): the incorporation of the orbital dynamics into the single-step parameterization, and the resulting factorized orbital-state CRB with its closed-form dynamics term.\n\nThis paper reverses the roles of the direct-geolocation configuration of [4,5,16,17]: instead of a satellite with known ephemeris locating an unknown ground emitter, ground stations at known positions determine the unknown six-dimensional orbital state of a non-cooperative satellite emitter. The contributions are:\n\n  * 1.\n\nA single-step estimator that determines the orbital state directly from the received signals in which the orbital-dynamics manifold is intrinsic to the parameterization. Every candidate is a valid orbit, so the entire trajectory, rather than a sequence of per-epoch positions, is searched through six orbital parameters (plus nuisances where present) independent of the arc length and the number of samples with no constraint penalty (Section 3);\n\n  * 2.\n\nA closed-form Cramér–Rao bound for the orbital state, with the light-time correction included in the delay model, that factors into signal, geometry, and dynamics terms, the dynamics entering as the two-body state-transition matrix (STM), with the Keplerian STM position block derived in closed form by implicit differentiation of the universal Kepler equation (Section 4 and Section 5);\n\n  * 3.\n\nNumerical validation of the derivation and known-waveform estimator efficiency, an observability analysis with a nondimensionalized state over several station layouts and passes, and a model-based comparison of the acquisition (wrong-peak) thresholds of the direct estimator and a two-step baseline that commits to hard per-window peak decisions (Section 7).\n\nThe approach is distinct from angle-only non-cooperative orbit determination (which uses optical bearings and two-step filtering) and POD (cooperative two-step); it is the direct RF single-step counterpart aimed at the weak and uncooperative regime.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDirection-of-arrival estimation in a mixture of K-distributed and Gaussian noise (https://digital.library.adelaide.edu.au/items/f7d51b2f-2662-4716-8f91-1dda27e64246)\nciteturn222search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # Direction-of-arrival estimation in a mixture of K-distributed and Gaussian noise ... We address the problem of estimating the directions-of-arrival (DoAs) of multiple signals received in the presence of a combination of a strong compound-Gaussian external noise and weak internal white Gaussian noise.Since the exact distribution of the mixture is not known, we get an insight into optimum procedure via a related model where we consider the texture of the compound-Gaussian component as an unknown and deterministic quantity to be estimated together with DoAs or a basis of the signal subspace.\n\n# Direction-of-arrival estimation in a mixture of K-distributed and Gaussian noise\n\n## Date\n\n2016\n\n## Authors\n\nBesson, O.\n\nAbramovich, Y.\n\nJohnson, B.\n\n## Type:\n\nJournal article\n\n## Citation\n\nSignal Processing, 2016; 128:512-520\n\n## DOI\n\n10.1016/j.sigpro.2016.05.027\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe address the problem of estimating the directions-of-arrival (DoAs) of multiple signals received in the presence of a combination of a strong compound-Gaussian external noise and weak internal white Gaussian noise. Since the exact distribution of the mixture is not known, we get an insight into optimum procedure via a related model where we consider the texture of the compound-Gaussian component as an unknown and deterministic quantity to be estimated together with DoAs or a basis of the signal subspace. Alternate maximization of the likelihood function is conducted and it is shown that it operates a separation between the snapshots with small/large texture values with respect to the additive noise power. The modified Cramér-Rao bound is derived and a prediction of the actual mean-square error is presented, based on separation between external/internal-noise dominated samples. Numerical simulations indicate that the suggested iterative DoA estimation technique comes close to the introduced bound and outperform a number of existing routines.\n\n## Rights\n\nCopyright 2016 Elsevier\n\n## Published Version\n\nhttp://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.sigpro.2016.05.027\n\n## Persistent link to this record\n\nhttps://hdl.handle.net/11541.2/123714\n\nFull item page--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Stochastic CramÉr–Rao Bound for Noncircular Signals with Application to DOA Estimation (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/3319053_Stochastic_CramEr-Rao_Bound_for_Noncircular_Signals_with_Application_to_DOA_Estimation)\nciteturn222search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 21.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; After providing an extension of the Slepian-Bangs formula for general noncircular complex Gaussian distributions, this paper focuses on the stochastic Crame´r-Rao bound (CRB) on direction-of-arrival (DOA) estimation accuracy for noncircular sources. ... In this paper, an algorithm of multiple signal classification (MUSIC) is proposed for two-dimensional (2-D) direction of- arrival (DOA) and polarization estimation of non-circular signal in three-dimensional (3-D) millimeter wave polarized largescale/ massive multiple-input-multiple-output (MIMO) systems. ... The stochastic Cramer-Rao Bound (CRB) for the estimation of the 2-D DOA and polarization parameters of the non-circular signals is derived as well.\n\n# Stochastic CramÉr–Rao Bound for Noncircular Signals with Application to DOA Estimation\n\n  * December 2004\n  * IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing 52(11):3192-3199\n\nDOI:10.1109/TSP.2004.836462\n\n  * Source\n  * IEEE Xplore\n\nAuthors:\n\nJ.-P. Delmas\n\n  * Institut Mines-Télécom\n\nHabti Abeida\n\n  * Taif University\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract and Figures\n\nAfter providing an extension of the Slepian-Bangs formula for general noncircular complex Gaussian distributions, this paper focuses on the stochastic Crame´r-Rao bound (CRB) on direction-of-arrival (DOA) estimation accuracy for noncircular sources. We derive an explicit expression of the CRB for DOA parameters alone in the case of noncircular complex Gaussian sources by two different methods. One of them consists of computing the asymptotic covariance matrix of the maximum likelihood (ML) estimator, and the other is obtained directly from our extended Slepian-Bangs formula. Some properties of this CRB are proved, and finally, it is numerically compared with the CRBs under circular complex Gaussian and complex discrete distributions of sources.\n\nRatio r = C = C… \n\nC… \n\n… \n\nRatio r = C = C… \n\n+3\n\nRatio r = C = C as a function of the noncircularity rate for different values of the SNR r .… \n\nFigures - uploaded by Habti Abeida\n\nAuthor content\n\nAll figure content in this area was uploaded by Habti Abeida\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nContent uploaded by Habti Abeida\n\nAuthor content\n\nAll content in this area was uploaded by Habti Abeida on Oct 27, 2015\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\n3192 IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SIGNAL PROCESSING, VOL. 52, NO. 11, NOVEMBER 2004\n\nStochastic Cramér–Rao Bound for Noncircular\n\nSignals with Application to DOA Estimation\n\nJean-Pierre Delmas, Member, IEEE, and Habti Abeida\n\nAbstract—After providing an extension of the Slepian–Bangs\n\nformula for general noncircular complex Gaussian distribu-\n\ntions, this paper focuses on the stochastic Cramér–Rao bound\n\n(CRB) on direction-of-arrival (DOA) estimation accuracy for\n\nWideband source signals occur in many applications of array signal processing. With the use of time-delay orthogonal representations of the incident uncorrelated wideband signals, the array output vector herein is divided into three parts. The first part is of the rank-1 form as if the incident signals are from the narrowband sources. The spatial signature of each signal is characterized by a direction of arrival (DOA) and second-order statistics dependent vector which can be viewed as the generalization of the steering vector. The second part contains the so-called virtual interferences which are uncorrelated with the signals. The third part is the sensor noise term. A deep learning based scheme is further developed for suppressing the virtual interference term in the covariance matrix of the array output. This enables a regime of time-domain wideband DOA estimation, using straightforwardly the subspace projection technique already developed under the narrowband assumption, with neither sub-band decomposition nor frequency focusing. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMaximum-likelihood direction of arrival estimation under intermittent jamming - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1051200421000671)\nciteturn222search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Cramer-Rao type performance lower bounds for the problem is also provided to illustrate the efficacy of the suggested estimator. ... Direction of arrival (DOA) estimation problem is a fundamental problem of statistical signal processing with close connections to frequency estimation, spectral estimation and signal modeling [1]. ... In [36], a deterministic method which uses alternating convex search for DOA estimation is formulated.\nCoherent signal model assumes that the phase difference between the coefficients of two consecutive snapshots are known a-priori which is an assumption compatible with the Swerling-1/3 target models in the radar signal processing literature. Non-coherent signal model does not have such an assumption and it is suitable for Swerling-2/4 targets. The suggested EM based solution is shown to yield an important estimation accuracy improvement over conventional maximum-likelihood solution which ignores the intermittency of jammer and also over the atomic norm based high resolution estimation techniques. Cramer-Rao type performance lower bounds for the problem is also provided to illustrate the efficacy of the suggested estimator.\n\n## Introduction\n\nDirection of arrival (DOA) estimation problem is a fundamental problem of statistical signal processing with close connections to frequency estimation, spectral estimation and signal modeling [1]. The DOA estimation under intermittent jamming, in spite of its importance in theory and practice, has attracted limited attention in open literature. The intermittent jamming model assumes that each one of the collected snapshot vectors is jammer corrupted with some probability. Stated differently, a subset of collected snapshots are contaminated by jammer and the others are jammer-free; but the receiver does not know which is which. The main goal of this work is to study the DOA estimation problem under intermittent jamming, that is to derive the performance lower bounds for this setting; to develop a maximum likelihood DOA estimation algorithm and to evaluate its performance by comparisons with the conventional maximum likelihood estimator which ignores the intermittent behavior and also with the sparse signal representation based high resolution estimators.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBayesian methods for sparse and low-rank matrix (https://www.diva-portal.org/smash/get/diva2%3A955100/FULLTEXT01.pdf)\nciteturn222search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Bayesian Cramér-Rao bounds for low-rank ... for unbiased estimators of deterministic parameters. ... A strong prior gives much information about thevariable while a weak prior gives less information about the parameters.\nChapter 7\nBayesian Cramér-Rao bounds for low-rank\nmatrix estimation\nI\nn the work of designing more accurate estimation algorithms, it is useful to\nknow how close (or far) the algorithms are from being optimal. Theoretical\nlower bounds provide limits on the Mean-Square Error (MSE) of estimators.\nWhen the performance of an estimator reaches the lower bound, we know that the\nestimator is optimal and that the bound is the best possible. When the performance\nof the best estimator and best lower bounds does not meet, it shows that there is\npossible to design better estimators and/or better bounds.\nThe Cramér-Rao bound [Cra47, Kay93] provides a lower bound on the MSE\nfor unbiased estimators of deterministic parameters. When the parameters to be\nestimated are random, the prior distributions need to be taken into account when\nderiving lower bounds. This is because the prior distribution brings additional in-\nformation about the parameters. A strong prior gives much information about the\nvariable while a weak prior gives less information about the parameters. For the ran-\ndom variables, a lower bound is given by the Bayesian Cramér-Rao Bound (BCRB)\n[VT04, VTB07, GL95, BS80]. In this chapter we investigate Bayesian Cramér-Rao\nbounds for certain Bayesian low-rank reconstruction models.\n7.1\nIntroduction\nIn low-rank matrix reconstruction (LRMR), we seek to estimate a low-rank matrix\nX ∈Rp×q from linear measurements\ny = A(X) + n = Avec(X) + n = Ax + n,\n(7.1)\nwhere y ∈Rm is the observed measurements, n ∈Rm is additive measurement noise\nand the sensing operator A : Rp×q →Rm and the sensing matrix A ∈Rm×pq are\ntwo equivalent representations of the linear sensing process. For brevity we intro-\nduce x ≜vec(X) where vec(·) is the standard vectorization operator. The sensing\n109--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDirection-of-Arrival Estimation: When MUSIC and ESPRIT Aren't Enough | New Leaf Tools (https://www.newleaftoolsllc.com/articles/direction-of-arrival-estimation-explained.html)\nciteturn222search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; A deterministic, CRB-efficient DOA estimator is one whose performance approaches the Cramér-Rao Lower Bound across the entire operating envelope — not just asymptotically, but in the finite-snapshot finite-SNR regime where real systems live. ... Drones are small, fly low, and often emit weak control-link signals at unpredictable times. ... The TSD DOA Engine is a deterministic, CRB-efficient direction-of-arrival estimator for ULAs from N=8 to N=64 elements.\n## The Cramér-Rao Lower Bound\n\nThe Cramér-Rao Lower Bound (CRB) is the theoretical minimum variance for any unbiased estimator of a parameter, given the signal model and the noise level. It is derived from the inverse of the Fisher Information Matrix and is independent of which algorithm you use.\n\nFor DOA estimation in a ULA with `L` snapshots and SNR `ρ`, the deterministic CRB scales as:\n\nCRB(θ) ~ 6 / (ρ · L · N(N^{2}−1) · cos^{2}(θ))\n\nThis says: the floor goes down with SNR, with the number of snapshots, with the cube of the number of elements, and with elevation angle. The CRB is the floor every estimator is bounded by.\n\nAn estimator is CRB-efficient if its variance equals the CRB. MUSIC is asymptotically CRB-efficient (efficient as `L → ∞` or `SNR → ∞`). In practice, with finite snapshots and finite SNR, MUSIC and ESPRIT both fall short of the CRB — sometimes by a large margin in the regions that matter most.\n\n## Where MUSIC and ESPRIT actually fail\n\nThe textbook says these algorithms work. ## What \"deterministic and CRB-efficient\" actually means\n\nA deterministic, CRB-efficient DOA estimator is one whose performance approaches the Cramér-Rao Lower Bound across the entire operating envelope — not just asymptotically, but in the finite-snapshot finite-SNR regime where real systems live. Specifically:\n\n  * No threshold tuning. No magic SNR number, no manually-set decision boundaries. The estimator either runs deterministically or it doesn't run.\n  * No snapshot-averaging assumption. The algorithm does not require the asymptotic limit; it works on the snapshots you have.\n  * No iterative convergence loop. The output is closed-form. Determinism is not \"we ran enough iterations\"; it is \"given the input, only this output is correct.\"\n  * Verifiable against MUSIC, ESPRIT, and the CRB. The estimator is benchmarked on a public dataset and the verification protocol is published. Anyone can reproduce the comparison independently.\n\nThe word deterministic here is doing real work. It does not mean \"the same input gives the same output\" (every numerical algorithm satisfies that trivially). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChapter 16. Performance Bounds and Statistical Analysis of DOA Estimation | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/285058928_Chapter_16_Performance_Bounds_and_Statistical_Analysis_of_DOA_Estimation)\nciteturn222search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; This paper focuses on the stochastic Cramer-Rao bound (CRB) of direction of arrival (DOA) estimates for binary phase-shift keying (BPSK) and quaternary phase-shift keying (QPSK) modulated signals corrupted by additive circular complex Gaussian noise. ... The main cause for this is that the MSE of several high-resolution direction of arrival algorithms are known to achieve the CRB under quite general/weak conditions. ... The strong consistency of the estimates of the number of signals, under different situations, is established. ... The results are applicable to the conditional (deterministic) signal model.\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nConditional and Unconditional Cramér–Rao Bounds for Near-Field Source Localization\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jun 2010\n\n  * Mohammed Nabil El Korso\n  * Remy Boyer\n  * Sylvie Marcos\n  * Alexandre Renaux\n\nNear-field source localization problem by a passive antenna array makes the assumption that the time-varying sources are located near the antenna. In this context, the far-field assumption (i.e., planar wavefront) is, of course, no longer valid and one has to consider a more complicated model parameterized by the bearing (as in the far-field case) and by the distance, named range, between the source and a reference coordinate system. One can find a plethora of estimation schemes in the literature, but their ultimate performance in terms of mean square error (MSE) have not been fully investigated. To characterize these performance, the Cramer-Rao bound (CRB) is a popular mathematical tool in signal processing. The main cause for this is that the MSE of several high-resolution direction of arrival algorithms are known to achieve the CRB under quite general/weak conditions. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNon-data-aided ML SNR estimation for AWGN channels with deterministic interference | Journal on Wireless Communications and Networking | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1687-1499-2014-45)\nciteturn222search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## 3 The Cramer-Rao bound\n## 3 The Cramer-Rao bound\n\n### 3.1 The real-valued system with PAM signaling\n\nIn this section, we derive the CRB for unbiased estimation of γ. Let θ= [ θ_{1},θ_{2}]=[A,σ^{2}] and . The CRB of SNR is given by [8], i.e.,\n\n(56)\n\nwhere\n\n(57)\n\nF is the Fisher information matrix (FIM) defined as\n\n(58)\n\nwhere E{·} denotes the expectation w.r.t the random variables {s_{ n }} and {v_{ n }}. After some algebra, we have\n\n\\begin{array}{ll}\\frac{\\mathrm{\\partial L}\\left(\\mathbf{x}\\right)}{\\partial {\\theta }_{\\ell }}& =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }^{2}}\\sum _{n=1}^{N}{\\varphi }_{\\ell }\\left({x}_{n}\\right),\\phantom{\\rule{1em}{0ex}}\\ell =1,2,\\phantom{\\rule{2em}{0ex}}\\end{array}\n\n(59)\n\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\varphi }_{1}\\left({x}_{n}\\right)& =({x}_{n}-I){H}_{n}^{s}-A{H}_{n}^{c}=({\\mathit{\\text{As}}}_{n}+{v}_{n}){H}_{n}^{s}-A{H}_{n}^{c},\\phantom{\\rule{2em}{0ex}}\\end{array}\n\n(60)\n\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\varphi